window.PROJECT_MCQ_BANKS.push({"bankId": "adult-education-june2020-review", "displayTitle": "Adult Education PYQs — June 2020 (Review draft)", "displaySubtitle": "100 questions with explanations. This is one paper from the planned 2,175-question v1.6 collection; flagged items are not scored.", "courseCode": "UGC NET · DRAFT", "sourceType": "page", "sourcePdf": "assets/adult-education-june2020.pdf", "questionCount": 100, "sections": [{"name": "June 2020 — Paper II", "count": 100}], "questions": [{"id": "adult-2020-06-II-001", "question": "Which one of the following is a manifest construct? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Self-esteem", "Mathematics", "Language", "Height"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "A manifest variable is directly observable or measurable. Height can be measured with an instrument, whereas self-esteem is a latent psychological construct inferred from indicators.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 1; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 1, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-002", "question": "Qualitative research involves various kinds of non-numerical data including: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Structured questionnaire", "Case studies", "Attitudes", "Multivariate techniques"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Case studies can provide detailed descriptions, interview accounts and contextual observations. Multivariate techniques are statistical procedures; an attitude is a construct rather than a particular qualitative research design.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 2; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 2, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-003", "question": "The first successful literacy campaign undertaken in independent India was: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Gram Shikshan Nilayam", "Gram Shikshan Mohim", "Gram Shikshan Maqam", "Gram Shikshan Jatha"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Gram Shikshan Mohim was the village education campaign associated with early mass adult-literacy work in Maharashtra. The distinguishing term is Mohim, meaning a campaign.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 3; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 3, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-004", "question": "What is the total number of UNESCO’s Pillars of Learning? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["4", "5", "6", "8"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The Delors Commission identified four pillars: learning to know, learning to do, learning to live together and learning to be.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 4; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 4, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-005", "question": "The National Adult Education Programme (NAEP) had the following three components: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Awareness, functional development and democracy", "Awareness, functional development and literacy", "Personality development, awareness and literacy", "Motivation, awareness and learning"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "NAEP combined literacy with functionality and awareness. Its purpose went beyond decoding written text to using learning in daily life and understanding social circumstances.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 5; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 5, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-006", "question": "Which one of the following is a traditional method of extension? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Radio", "Nukkad-Natak", "Television", "Social media"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Nukkad-Natak is street theatre, a folk or traditional communication method. Radio, television and social media depend on electronic or digital channels.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 6; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 6, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-007", "question": "In the history of adult education in India, Shramik Vidyapeeths were renamed as: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Jana Shikshan Sangathan", "Jana Shikshan Sansthan", "Jana Shikshan Sangreh", "Jana Shikshan Sanathan"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Shramik Vidyapeeths became Jan Shikshan Sansthans. The programme links vocational skills and livelihood improvement with learning opportunities for disadvantaged adults; the options retain the book’s spelling.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 7; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 7, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-008", "question": "What was the total number of religious minorities notified by the Government of India at the time of this examination? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["3", "4", "5", "6"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The six nationally notified religious minority communities were Muslims, Christians, Sikhs, Buddhists, Parsis and Jains. This is an exam-time institutional fact, rather than a count of every minority group in India.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 8; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 8, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-009", "question": "After the MDGs, how many Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) were adopted by the United Nations? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["15", "16", "17", "18"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The 2030 Agenda contains 17 Sustainable Development Goals. These succeeded the eight Millennium Development Goals; the number of goals must not be confused with the larger number of targets.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 9; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 9, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 1, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-010", "question": "“Inquiry, listening, perception, retention, reflection, inference and steadfast adherence to conclusions are the quality of the intellect.” This quotation is attributed to: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["V. K. R. V. Rao", "Kautilya", "M. K. Gandhi", "B. R. Ambedkar"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The printed key attributes this description of intellectual qualities to Kautilya. The list concerns acquiring, retaining and reasoning about knowledge. The exact wording of the translated quotation has not been independently verified.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 10; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 10, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 1, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-011", "question": "Formal education covers which one of the following aspects? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Any location, including on the job", "Common structure syllabus", "Education is future-time oriented", "Education is short-term in period"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The book selects future-time orientation: formal schooling typically prepares learners for later stages of education and work. Option B is also compatible with formal education, so the wording is not sufficiently exclusive for an unambiguous single-answer item.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 11; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 11, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 1, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-012", "question": "The Adult Education Committee was appointed in India in which year? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["1947", "1952", "1968", "1938"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The printed key identifies 1938. The question refers to the pre-independence adult-education committee, rather than the post-independence education commissions or the 1978 NAEP. The committee is not named in the question, so this historical attribution needs source verification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 12; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 12, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 2, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-013", "question": "Who worked as a Samagra Gram Sevak and started a grain bank in the village? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Shrimati Kasturaben", "Shrimati Durgaben", "Shrimati Sarojini Naidu", "Shrimati Narulkar"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The book identifies Shrimati Narulkar. A grain bank illustrates community self-help: grain is pooled or stored locally to support households when supplies are scarce. The personal attribution is retained from the printed key and requires independent verification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 13; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 13, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 2, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-014", "question": "Which one of the following is an intervening variable? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Test scores", "Adult intervention", "Crime", "Attention"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Attention can mediate the effect of an educational intervention on learning outcomes. For example, an intervention may improve attention, which then improves test scores. A variable’s role ultimately depends on the research model.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 14; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 14, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-015", "question": "Who stated, “Without a revolutionary theory, there can be no revolutionary movement”? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Paulo Freire", "Vladimir Lenin", "Louis Althusser", "Ivan Illich"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The statement is associated with Lenin’s What Is to Be Done? It expresses his view that revolutionary political action requires theoretical guidance.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 15; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 15, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-016", "question": "In which year did the Government start Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA), with the objective of providing education to children aged 6–14 years by 2010? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["2001", "2005", "2000", "2002"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "SSA became operational in 2000–01 and is commonly dated to 2001 in examination questions. Its focus was universal elementary education for children aged 6–14.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 16; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 16, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-017", "question": "Identify the components of an evidence-based adult education system. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Basic and applied research providing evidence to build programme models", "Evaluation testing the effectiveness of programme models", "Practitioner knowledge improving implementation of programme models", "All the above"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Research develops and tests ideas, evaluation examines programme performance, and practitioner knowledge informs implementation. An evidence-based system uses these complementary sources rather than relying on only one.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 17; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 17, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-018", "question": "Which theories did McGregor propose for managing people? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Theory X and Z", "Theory K and L", "Theory X and Y", "Theory A and B"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "McGregor contrasted Theory X, based on assumptions that workers avoid work and need control, with Theory Y, which allows for self-direction, responsibility and intrinsic motivation under suitable conditions.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 18; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 18, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-019", "question": "The term learning strategy is often used to refer to: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["A pattern of actions of an individual who uses learning skills and resources", "A pattern of reflections for a situation", "Response to the environment", "Steps of an individual in the learning process"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "A learning strategy is a purposeful pattern of actions through which a learner selects and uses skills and resources. Merely responding to the environment does not necessarily involve strategic planning.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 19; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 19, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-020", "question": "The Institute for Retired Professionals, instituted by Hy Hirsch, is located in which country? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Canada", "United Kingdom", "USA", "France"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The Institute for Retired Professionals was established at The New School in New York. It is an example of peer learning and continuing intellectual engagement in later life.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 20; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 20, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-021", "question": "Who stated that education can never be neutral in the context of community? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Ivan Illich", "Ralph W. Tyler", "Paulo Freire", "Peter Jarvis"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Freire treated education as a political and ethical practice. It can reproduce existing relations of power or help learners critically understand and transform their circumstances.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 21; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 21, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-022", "question": "What is the title of the report of the International Commission on the Development of Education? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Learning for Development", "Learning and Development", "Learning to Do", "Learning to Be"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The Faure Commission’s 1972 report was Learning to Be. Distinguish it from the Delors Commission’s 1996 report, Learning: The Treasure Within.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 22; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 22, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-023", "question": "Who envisaged a university as standing for humanism, tolerance, reason, progress, the adventure of ideas and the search for truth? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Lal Bahadur Shastri", "Jawaharlal Nehru", "Indira Gandhi", "Manmohan Singh"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "This formulation is associated with Jawaharlal Nehru’s conception of a university. It stresses free inquiry and humanistic values alongside the pursuit of knowledge.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 23; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 23, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-024", "question": "Which Sustainable Development Goal covers lifelong learning? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Good Health and Well-being", "Quality Education", "Gender Equality", "Decent Work"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "SDG 4 calls for inclusive and equitable quality education and lifelong learning opportunities for all. Lifelong learning therefore extends the goal beyond school-age education.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 24; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 24, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-025", "question": "A faculty gained by knowledge or practice is known as: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Value", "Perfection", "Skill", "None of the above"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A skill is an acquired capacity to perform an activity. Practice and knowledge develop that capacity; a value is a belief about what is desirable, and perfection is an ideal standard.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 25; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 25, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 2, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-026", "question": "The draft National Education Policy 2019 proposed achieving a 100% youth and adult literacy rate by which year? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["2025", "2030", "2035", "2040"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The 2019 draft policy proposed universal youth and adult literacy by 2030. This is a policy target, not evidence that the target was achieved.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 26; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Draft NEP 2019: https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/Draft_NEP_2019_EN_Revised.pdf", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 26, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-027", "question": "A method for determining a parameter of an unknown population is known as: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Estimator", "Sampling", "Forecasting", "Assumption"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "An estimator is a rule or statistic used to estimate a population parameter from sample data. For example, the sample mean estimates the population mean; sampling is the process of selecting observations.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 27; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 27, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-028", "question": "Who believed that, in genuine dialogue, the teacher teaches the learner, who learns and also teaches the teacher? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Nell Emmen", "Paulo Freire", "Arthur Clarke", "Sir Bernard Crick"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Freire’s dialogical education treats teachers and learners as participants in a shared process of inquiry. This challenges the banking model in which a teacher merely deposits knowledge in passive learners.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 28; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 28, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-029", "question": "Non-formal education is: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Long-term", "Future-time oriented", "Present-time oriented", "Dependent on classroom teaching"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Non-formal education is often organised around learners’ immediate needs, such as literacy or livelihood skills. It can be flexible in location and duration and need not take place in a conventional classroom.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 29; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 29, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-030", "question": "Which characteristic is associated with a traditional classroom? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Tutor leads", "Classroom optional", "Uniform delivery", "Self-paced"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The printed answer is tutor-led teaching, reflecting the central instructional role of the teacher. Uniform delivery can also describe a traditional classroom, making option C a plausible alternative under this broad wording.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 30; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 30, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-031", "question": "Long, medium and fast-track approaches are used in: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Non-formal education", "Formal education", "ICT in education", "Distance education"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The book selects ICT in education. These labels describe different implementation timeframes, but the question does not identify the framework that defines them. The answer needs verification against the intended framework.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 31; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 31, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 3, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-032", "question": "Where and when was the Third International Conference on Adult Education held? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Paris, 1985", "Montreal, 1960", "Denmark, 1949", "Tokyo, 1972"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "CONFINTEA III took place in Tokyo in 1972. The alternatives correspond to other conferences: Elsinore, Denmark, in 1949; Montreal in 1960; and Paris in 1985.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 32; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 32, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-033", "question": "The failure of formal education is attributed in this question to: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Teachers are not trained", "Funds are not sufficient", "Non-cooperation from people", "A majority of children have not enrolled"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The printed key emphasises failure to reach children outside the formal system. The point concerns access and exclusion, not a claim that this is the only cause of educational failure or a current enrolment statistic.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 33; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 33, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-034", "question": "In which year did Cambridge University first use the term extension education, according to the examination’s historical convention? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["1873", "1920", "1902", "1952"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "1873 is associated with the establishment of Cambridge’s university extension lecture scheme. The question uses that institutional milestone to date extension education; earlier uses of the term are a separate historical issue.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 34; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 34, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-035", "question": "The Open School System was introduced at the national level during which Five-Year Plan? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Fifth Plan", "Seventh Plan", "Tenth Plan", "Ninth Plan"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The National Open School was established in 1989, during the Seventh Five-Year Plan (1985–90). The book prints C, but the Tenth Plan (2002–07) relates to a later period and does not fit establishment of the national institution.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 35; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Book key: C; proposed correction: B. Verify against NIOS institutional history before release. Verified institutional history: https://digital.nios.ac.in/about.php", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 35, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Book key: C; proposed correction: B. Verify against NIOS institutional history before release."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-036", "question": "Which characteristic is NOT associated with traditional classroom teaching? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Quality is independent of the tutor", "Book-assisted teaching", "Tutor leads", "Only group-paced learning"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Traditional classroom teaching depends substantially on the teacher’s instructional skill. Its quality therefore cannot be assumed to be independent of the tutor. Option A has been reconstructed from the book’s awkward wording.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 36; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 36, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-037", "question": "Which is the apex body for curriculum-related matters in school education in India? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["CBSE", "NCTE", "NIOS", "NCERT"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "NCERT provides national academic advice and develops curricular frameworks and educational resources for schools. CBSE is an examination board, NCTE regulates teacher education, and NIOS provides open schooling.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 37; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 37, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-038", "question": "The modern higher education system in India is approximately how old, according to this question? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["70 years", "50 years", "150 years", "60 years"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The conventional reference point is the establishment of the universities of Calcutta, Bombay and Madras in 1857. The book’s approximate 150-year description should be read historically, not treated as an exact age in 2020 or today.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 38; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 38, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-039", "question": "Statement I: Adolescents have peculiar characteristics and undergo rapid physical, emotional and mental changes.\nStatement II: Adolescence, though difficult, is an important phase of growth and development.\nChoose the most appropriate answer. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect", "Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Adolescence involves major physical, cognitive and psychosocial changes. Both statements describe its developmental significance; calling it important does not mean that every adolescent experiences the same difficulties.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 39; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 39, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-040", "question": "Statement I: The Community Development Programme was a major post-independence programme for improving people’s socio-economic conditions.\nStatement II: National Extension Service was launched on 2 October 1953.\nChoose the most appropriate answer. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect", "Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The Community Development Programme began on 2 October 1952. National Extension Service followed on 2 October 1953 to extend the community-development approach more widely. The book describes the former as the biggest programme of its kind.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 40; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 40, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 3, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-041", "question": "Assertion A: A research hypothesis is a tentative answer to a question.\nReason R: As soon as a research question is formulated, it makes a hypothesis imperative, since it is an intelligent guess about the research question under study. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "A hypothesis is a tentative, testable answer. However, not every research question requires a formal hypothesis: exploratory and some qualitative studies can proceed without one. The book selects B, but the absolute claim in R makes C the defensible answer.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 41; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. Book: B; proposed correction: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 41, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 4, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. Book: B; proposed correction: C."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-042", "question": "Assertion A: The judiciary has played a very important role in the restoration of social justice in India.\nReason R: The Constitution of India mandates the state to accord socio-economic and political justice to all its citizens. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The printed key treats both statements as true but does not accept R as a direct explanation of the judiciary’s particular role. R states a constitutional obligation of the state broadly. The relationship is interpretive, so retain the key with this qualification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 42; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 42, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 4, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-043", "question": "Assertion A: There is a visible bias based on gender.\nReason R: Patriarchy is deeply rooted in society. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Patriarchal institutions and norms distribute authority and opportunities unequally by gender. They therefore provide an explanation for the gender bias asserted in A.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 43; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 43, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 4, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-044", "question": "Statement I: India is fully equipped to deal with any kind of disaster.\nStatement II: Climate change and global warming put substantial pressure on poor communities. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect", "Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The absolute claim of complete preparedness for every disaster is untenable. Poor communities can face high exposure, insecure livelihoods and limited capacity to adapt, supporting Statement II.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 44; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 44, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 4, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-045", "question": "Assertion A: One District One Product is a focus of the government.\nReason R: The government has started various schemes for employment generation in India, including Make in India and Startup India. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The two statements concern government initiatives, but the existence of other employment schemes does not itself explain the district-specific product approach of ODOP. Hence the printed key accepts both while rejecting the explanatory link.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 45; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 45, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 4, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-046", "question": "Arrange the learning ladder:\n(a) Knowledge\n(b) Insight\n(c) Information\n(d) Data\n(e) Understanding (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["d, c, e, b, a", "d, b, c, a, e", "b, e, a, c, d", "d, c, a, e, b"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The proposed progression is data → information → knowledge → understanding → insight. Each step involves greater interpretation or integration of what was available at the preceding step.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 46; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 46, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-047", "question": "Arrange the steps in evaluating students’ performance:\n(a) Consider educational objectives\n(b) Interpret results\n(c) Make a judgement\n(d) Use a testing tool for assessment\n(e) Consider learning outcomes (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["e, a, c, d, b", "a, e, d, b, c", "c, a, e, d, b", "b, d, c, e, a"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Clarify objectives and expected outcomes before choosing assessment tools. Interpret the resulting evidence and then make an evaluative judgement; judgement should not precede assessment.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 47; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 47, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-048", "question": "Arrange these phases of instruction:\n(a) Inform learners of objectives\n(b) Present stimulus material\n(c) Provide learning guidance\n(d) Gain attention\n(e) Stimulate recall of prerequisite learning (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["d, a, e, b, c", "a, d, b, e, c", "d, a, b, c, e", "e, d, c, b, a"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "These are the opening events in Gagné’s instructional sequence: gain attention, communicate objectives, activate prior learning, present the material and provide guidance.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 48; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 48, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-049", "question": "Arrange the steps of experiential learning:\n(a) Abstract conceptualisation\n(b) Concrete experience\n(c) Active experimentation\n(d) Reflective observation (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c, d", "b, d, a, c", "c, d, a, b", "d, c, b, a"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Kolb’s cycle moves from concrete experience to reflection, then to conceptual understanding and active experimentation. The experiment generates experiences that can begin another cycle.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 49; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 49, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-050", "question": "Arrange the qualitative data-analysis process:\n(a) Prepare data\n(b) Present data in narrative form\n(c) Organise data\n(d) Code data (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["d, c, a, b", "a, d, c, b", "d, a, b, c", "a, c, d, b"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Prepare and organise the material before systematic coding, then communicate the analysis in narrative form. Real qualitative analysis can be iterative; the item asks for this simplified workflow.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 50; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 50, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-051", "question": "Arrange the steps for interpreting a regression coefficient:\n(a) State the predicted change in the dependent variable for a one-unit change in the independent variable\n(b) Describe a one-unit increase in the independent variable\n(c) Identify the independent variable and its units\n(d) Identify the dependent variable and its units (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["c, b, d, a", "a, d, c, b", "b, d, a, c", "d, c, b, a"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "First establish the predictor’s units and the meaning of a one-unit increase, then identify the outcome’s units and express the associated predicted change. In multiple regression, the interpretation also holds other included predictors constant.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 51; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 51, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 5, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-052", "question": "Arrange the learning process according to the Jarvis model:\n(a) Situation\n(b) Reinforced\n(c) Practice experimentation\n(d) The person\n(e) Experience (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["e, b, a, d, c", "b, a, d, c, e", "d, a, e, b, c", "a, c, b, e, d"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The printed sequence begins with a person in a situation who has an experience, followed by reinforcement and practice/experimentation. Jarvis’s model contains several possible learning routes, so this simplified sequence should not be mistaken for its complete structure.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 52; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 52, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 5, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-053", "question": "Arrange these factors as a causal pathway:\n(a) Swine flu\n(b) Programme\n(c) Access to masks\n(d) Knowledge (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["d, c, a, b", "a, c, b, d", "b, d, c, a", "b, a, c, d"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The programme is the intervention, knowledge is an intermediate change, access to masks is a further mechanism, and swine-flu outcomes are the endpoint. The pathway expresses a proposed mechanism, not proof that the programme caused the outcome.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 53; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 53, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-054", "question": "Arrange the steps from an idea to action:\n(a) Organisation and planning\n(b) Legitimisation\n(c) Idea initiation\n(d) Action (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c, d", "c, b, a, d", "d, a, b, c", "b, c, d, a"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "An idea is initiated, gains acceptance or legitimacy, is organised into a plan, and is then implemented. Planning and action depend on an accepted proposal.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 54; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 54, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-055", "question": "Arrange the problem-solving steps:\n(a) Implement the solution\n(b) Choose an appropriate alternative\n(c) Clearly define the problem\n(d) Look for solutions and the normal sequence of events (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["b, c, d, a", "c, d, b, a", "a, b, d, c", "b, a, d, c"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Define the problem before generating possible solutions. Compare alternatives, choose an appropriate one and then implement it. This prevents premature action on a poorly specified problem.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 55; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 55, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-056", "question": "Which are activity aids?\n(a) Science museum\n(b) Aquarium\n(c) Epidiascope\n(d) Nature study garden (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "c and d only", "a, b and c only", "a, b and d only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Museums, aquaria and nature gardens provide opportunities for observation and experiential activity. An epidiascope is a projection device, so it belongs to projected visual aids.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 56; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 56, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-057", "question": "What are the criteria for effective organisation of learning experiences?\n(a) Sequence\n(b) Continuity\n(c) Series\n(d) Integration (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and c only", "b and d only", "a, b and d only", "b, c and d only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Tyler’s criteria are continuity, sequence and integration. Continuity revisits important elements, sequence develops them progressively, and integration connects learning across areas.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 57; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 57, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-058", "question": "The term learning experience includes:\n(a) Feedback\n(b) Interaction with external conditions and the environment\n(c) Teacher’s explanation\n(d) Teaching aids\n(e) Summative assessment (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and c only", "a and b only", "a, b, c and d only", "c and e only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The book uses a broad meaning that includes interaction, feedback, explanations and aids encountered by a learner. Strictly, a learning experience concerns what the learner does in interaction with conditions; a teacher’s explanation or an aid alone does not guarantee learning.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 58; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 58, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 6, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-059", "question": "What are common features of measurement scales?\n(a) A series of stimuli\n(b) Fixed and limited response choices\n(c) Numerical scoring of responses\n(d) Depends on responding to suitable statements (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "a, b and c only", "c and d only", "b and d only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Standardised rating scales typically present stimuli or items, specify response categories, and assign numerical scores. The question uses the instrument sense of scale, rather than the nominal/ordinal/interval/ratio classification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 59; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 59, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-060", "question": "Which approaches went hand in hand in planning adult-education programmes?\n(a) Selective approach\n(b) Mass approach\n(c) Individual approach\n(d) Community approach (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "c and d only", "b and c only", "a and d only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The selective approach targets particular groups or areas, whereas the mass approach seeks broad participation. The printed answer treats these as complementary planning approaches.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 60; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 60, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 6, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-061", "question": "Which were Gandhi’s experimental laboratories of adult education?\n(a) Phoenix\n(b) Champaran\n(c) Sewagram\n(d) Aurobindo\n(e) Kosbad (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "c and d only", "b and e only", "b, c and e only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Phoenix Settlement, Champaran and Sevagram are associated with Gandhi’s constructive work and educational experiments. Aurobindo is associated with a different educational tradition; Kosbad is associated with other community-education pioneers.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 61; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 61, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-062", "question": "Gandhian adult education trains individuals to sacrifice for which wider communities?\n(a) Family and later the village\n(b) Village for the district\n(c) District for the province\n(d) A political party for the nation\n(e) Nation for all (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "a, b and c only", "b, c and d only", "a, b, c and e only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The sequence widens social responsibility from family and village to district, province and humanity. Loyalty to a political party is not the equivalent moral step in this formulation.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 62; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 62, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-063", "question": "What are basic components of Freire’s literacy method?\n(a) Decodification in a culture circle under a self-effacing stimulus\n(b) People paying attention to instructors’ explanations\n(c) A search for generative words\n(d) Tuning in to the people’s vernacular universe (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "c and d only", "a, c and d only", "b and d only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Freire begins with learners’ language and lived conditions, uses generative words and themes, and promotes critical dialogue in culture circles. Passive reception of an instructor’s explanation is inconsistent with this participatory emphasis.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 63; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 63, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-064", "question": "Philosophy of education philosophically studies which aspects?\n(a) Purpose of education\n(b) Duration of education\n(c) Process of education\n(d) Nature and ideals of education (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["b and c only", "c and d only", "a, b and c only", "a, c and d only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Philosophical inquiry examines what education is, what it should aim to achieve, and how its practices can be justified. Duration by itself is mainly an organisational matter.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 64; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 64, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-065", "question": "Which are non-projected visual aids?\n(a) Marker board\n(b) Puppet\n(c) Slides\n(d) Flashcards (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["b, c and d only", "a, b and c only", "a, c and d only", "a, b and d only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "Boards, puppets and flashcards can be viewed directly. Slides are normally classified as projected aids because their display uses a projector or display system.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 65; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 65, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-066", "question": "Identify advantages of teaching aids.\n(a) They supplement verbal instruction\n(b) They provide variety\n(c) They should be simple and brief\n(d) They save time and energy (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "b, c and d only", "a, b and d only", "a, c and d only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Supplementing speech, providing variety and saving time are potential benefits. Being simple and brief is a design recommendation, rather than an advantage resulting from use.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 66; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 66, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-067", "question": "Select the non-probability sampling methods.\n(a) Incidental\n(b) Purposive\n(c) Cluster\n(d) Quota (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "a, b and d only", "b, c and d only", "a, c and d only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Incidental, purposive and quota sampling do not require known random selection probabilities. Conventional cluster sampling selects clusters through a probability mechanism.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 67; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 67, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 7, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-068", "question": "Choose the principles of curriculum development.\n(a) It should be dynamic\n(b) It is a broad and comprehensive process\n(c) Mobility\n(d) Problem-oriented (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "b, c and d only", "a, c and d only", "a, b and d only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The book selects dynamism, comprehensiveness and mobility. However, problem orientation is also a recognised curricular emphasis, and the item does not name a framework that excludes it. Keep this answer qualified pending verification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 68; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 68, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 8, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-069", "question": "Identify advantages of an inventory.\n(a) Useful in vocational guidance\n(b) Individual and group administration are possible\n(c) A resource to reconnect to employees\n(d) It is not very time-bound (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and d only", "b, c and d only", "a, c and d only", "a, b and c only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Interest and personality inventories can support vocational guidance and can often be administered individually or in groups without a speed-test emphasis. Reconnecting to employees is not a general defining advantage of such inventories.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 69; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 69, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 8, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-070", "question": "Select the main functions of communication.\n(a) Change behaviour\n(b) Guide change\n(c) Persuasive communication\n(d) Informative communication (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and c only", "a and d only", "a and b only", "c and d only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The printed key uses an extension-education perspective: communication helps bring about and guide change. Informing and persuading are also standard communication functions, so the broad wording makes D defensible in another framework.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 70; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 70, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 8, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-071", "question": "Professionalisation of adult education includes:\n(a) Defining\n(b) Forming academics\n(c) Producing literature\n(d) State support\n(e) Graduate study (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, c and d only", "b, c and d only", "a, b, c and e only", "b, c, d and e only"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The printed answer emphasises defining the field, developing an academic community and literature, and providing graduate study. “Forming academics” is retained from the scan; state support can also assist professionalisation, so the intended classification needs verification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 71; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 71, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 8, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-072", "question": "Besides inclusive, equitable quality education and lifelong learning, which areas are included in the UN Sustainable Development Goals?\n(a) Poverty\n(b) Health\n(c) Energy\n(d) Land\n(e) Water (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c and d only", "a, c and d only", "b, c and d only", "a, b, c and e only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The book selects poverty, health, energy and water. However, SDG 15 is Life on Land, so land is also covered by the SDGs. All five areas are included, and none of the offered combinations is fully correct.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 72; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. No fully defensible offered answer; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 72, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 8, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. No fully defensible offered answer; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-073", "question": "Which lists contain recognised models of communication?\n(a) Shannon–Weaver, Schramm, Berlo, Karl Marx\n(b) Berlo, Shannon–Weaver, Schramm, Aristotle\n(c) Shannon–Weaver, Lasswell, Berlo, Schramm\n(d) Schramm, Berlo, Aristotle, Marx (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "b and c only", "c and d only", "b and d only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Shannon–Weaver, Schramm, Berlo, Aristotle and Lasswell are standard names in introductory communication-model classifications. The lists containing Marx do not fit that particular classification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 73; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 73, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 8, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-074", "question": "Value-based education includes:\n(a) Attitudes towards self\n(b) Attitudes towards others\n(c) Attitudes towards social groups to which one belongs\n(d) Attitudes towards children (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "b, c and d only", "a, b and d only", "a, c and d only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The printed classification includes attitudes towards self, other people and social groups. Attitudes towards children are a specific instance within attitudes towards others; this explains the broader grouping used in the key.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 74; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 74, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 8, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-075", "question": "Which statements about educational research are true?\n(a) A research problem leads towards dependable solutions\n(b) It provides direction for the choice of research design\n(c) It contributes to useful generalisations and theory development\n(d) It limits the scope for any error (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and d only", "a, b and c only", "b, c and d only", "a, c and d only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "A clearly defined problem guides design and systematic inquiry can support solutions and theory development. Research does not eliminate every possible error, so the absolute claim in d is inappropriate.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 75; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 75, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 9, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-076", "question": "The broad goals of post-literacy and continuing education may be translated into which objectives?\n(a) Reconciliation\n(b) Remediation\n(c) Continuation\n(d) Application\n(e) Communication (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c and d only", "b, c, d and e only", "a, c, d and e only", "a, d and e only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Post-literacy work remedies gaps, sustains further learning, encourages application of acquired skills and supports communication. Reconciliation is not one of the objectives in this classification.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 76; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 76, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 9, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-077", "question": "Which are UNESCO’s four pillars of learning?\n(a) Learning to know\n(b) Learning to swim\n(c) Learning to live together\n(d) Learning to be\n(e) Learning to do (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c and d only", "b, c, d and e only", "a, b, d and e only", "a, c, d and e only"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The Delors framework comprises learning to know, do, live together and be. Learning to swim may be a useful skill, but it is not the name of a pillar.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 77; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 77, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 9, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-078", "question": "Which are essential components of adult education in this classification?\n(a) Post-vocational education\n(b) Education for deprived classes\n(c) Education for illiterate people\n(d) Education in life skills\n(e) Education for overseas employment (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b, c and d only", "b, c, d and e only", "a, c, d and e only", "a, b, d and e only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The first four cover continuing vocational development, inclusion, literacy and life skills. Preparing people specifically for overseas employment is a possible specialised course rather than an essential component of all adult education.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 78; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 78, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 9, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-079", "question": "Which are recognised types of educational research?\n(a) Descriptive research\n(b) Simple research\n(c) Experimental research\n(d) Qualitative research\n(e) Complex research (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a, b and c only", "a, c and d only", "b, c and d only", "c, d and e only"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Descriptive, experimental and qualitative research are recognised categories. “Simple” and “complex” describe difficulty or structure but do not specify standard methodological types.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 79; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 79, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 9, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-080", "question": "Integrated approaches to lifelong learning include:\n(a) Mass education\n(b) Social inclusion\n(c) Elementary education\n(d) Higher education (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["a and b only", "c and d only", "a and d only", "b and c only"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The printed key emphasises participation at scale and social inclusion. Elementary and higher education are institutional stages that can also form part of lifelong learning, so the answer depends on reading “approaches” as distinct from educational stages.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 80; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 80, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 9, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-081", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) ILO\n(b) IIEP\n(c) IIALM\n(d) ICAE\nList II:\n1. Tehran\n2. Toronto\n3. Paris\n4. Geneva\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["2, 1, 3, 4", "3, 2, 4, 1", "4, 3, 1, 2", "1, 4, 2, 3"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The historical matches are ILO–Geneva; UNESCO IIEP–Paris; International Institute for Adult Literacy Methods–Tehran; and International Council for Adult Education–Toronto. The last two should be read as historical institutional facts for this question, not assumed to describe present headquarters.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 81; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 81, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 9, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-082", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Communication\n(b) Oral communication\n(c) Visual media\n(d) Experimental World Literacy Programme\nList II:\n1. Drama\n2. 1965\n3. Speed of delivery\n4. Telephonic communication\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["2, 3, 4, 1", "3, 4, 1, 2", "4, 1, 2, 3", "1, 2, 3, 4"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Telephone conversation is an oral channel; drama supplies a visual performance; the Experimental World Literacy Programme is associated with 1965. Speed of delivery is the remaining communication characteristic in the matching scheme.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 82; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 82, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 10, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-083", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Evaluating Functional Literacy\n(b) Inequality\n(c) Meeting the Basic Needs of the Rural Poor\n(d) Compulsory Miseducation and The Community of Scholars\nList II:\n1. Philip H. Coombs\n2. Paul Goodman\n3. André Béteille\n4. H. S. Bhola\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["1, 2, 3, 4", "3, 1, 2, 4", "4, 3, 1, 2", "2, 4, 3, 1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The associations are Bhola with functional-literacy evaluation, Béteille with inequality, Coombs with rural basic needs, and Goodman with Compulsory Miseducation and The Community of Scholars. Book-title typography has been normalised.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 83; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 83, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 10, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-084", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Qualitative research\n(b) Field experiments\n(c) Longitudinal studies\n(d) Sampling method\nList II:\n1. Whole group\n2. Snowball techniques\n3. Multiple realities\n4. Coherent studies\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["4, 2, 3, 1", "1, 3, 4, 2", "2, 4, 1, 3", "3, 1, 2, 4"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The printed answer would match longitudinal studies to snowball techniques and sampling to “coherent studies”, which is not defensible. Snowball is a sampling technique; longitudinal research follows change over time. The last phrase may be a misprint for cohort studies, but even that correction does not produce a valid offered code.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 84; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. No fully defensible offered answer; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 84, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 10, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring. No fully defensible offered answer; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-085", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) World Education Forum\n(b) State Resource Centre\n(c) National Literacy Mission\n(d) Assisted by the World Bank\nList II:\n1. 1988\n2. District Primary Education Programme (DPEP)\n3. Dakar, 2000\n4. Agency for open distance learning\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["1, 2, 3, 4", "2, 3, 4, 1", "3, 4, 1, 2", "4, 1, 2, 3"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The World Education Forum met at Dakar in 2000; the National Literacy Mission began in 1988; DPEP received World Bank assistance. The remaining match assigns State Resource Centres a learning-support role, although their usual adult-education remit is broader than open distance learning.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 85; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 85, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 10, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-086", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Problem of non-formal education\n(b) Formal education\n(c) E-content development scheme\n(d) Strengthening higher education\nList II:\n1. University Grants Commission\n2. Lack of community participation\n3. Tenth Plan period\n4. Rigidly classroom-centred\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["2, 4, 1, 3", "3, 1, 2, 4", "4, 2, 3, 1", "1, 3, 4, 2"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The matching scheme links non-formal programme difficulties to weak community participation, formal education to classroom organisation, e-content development to UGC, and higher-education strengthening to the Tenth Plan. These are contextual associations, not exclusive definitions.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 86; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 86, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 10, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-087", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Teacher-education curriculum\n(b) World Initiative on Lifelong Learning (WILL)\n(c) Computer\n(d) Teaching method\nList II:\n1. A people-networking tool\n2. NCERT\n3. Demonstration\n4. Canada\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["1, 3, 4, 2", "2, 4, 1, 3", "3, 1, 2, 4", "4, 2, 3, 1"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The printed scheme matches teacher-education curriculum with NCERT, WILL with Canada, computers with networking, and demonstration with teaching method. The WILL attribution should be checked against the historical initiative intended by the question.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 87; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 87, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 11, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-088", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Turning words into a number\n(b) Using qualitative evidence to illustrate statistical results\n(c) Case studies\n(d) Interpretative studies of texts\nList II:\n1. Exploratory analysis\n2. Content analysis\n3. Qualitative analysis\n4. Quantitative analysis\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["4, 2, 3, 1", "2, 4, 1, 3", "1, 3, 2, 4", "4, 1, 3, 2"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The book’s intended matches are coding verbal material in content analysis, quantitative results illustrated by qualitative evidence, exploratory case studies, and qualitative textual interpretation. The categories overlap: case studies need not be exploratory, and content analysis may be quantitative or qualitative.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 88; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Answer requires review; excluded from scoring.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 88, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 11, "unscored": true, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Answer requires review; excluded from scoring."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-089", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Children\n(b) Women\n(c) Foetus\n(d) Elderly\nList II:\n1. Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace Act, 2013\n2. Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act, 2012\n3. Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act, 2007\n4. Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques Act, 1994\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["3, 1, 2, 4", "2, 3, 4, 1", "4, 2, 3, 1", "2, 1, 4, 3"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The pairs identify the populations or concerns addressed by the named laws: children–POCSO; women–workplace sexual harassment; prenatal sex selection–PCPNDT; older persons–maintenance and welfare. The book’s “POSCO” spelling has been corrected to POCSO.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 89; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D. Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 89, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 11, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Wording reconstructed or normalised from the scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-090", "question": "Match List I with List II.\nList I:\n(a) Directive Principles of State Policy\n(b) Kothari Commission\n(c) Mid-Day Meal Scheme\n(d) National Literacy Mission\nList II:\n1. 1966\n2. 1995\n3. 1950\n4. 1988\nOptions give the matches for (a), (b), (c), (d), respectively. (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["3, 4, 2, 1", "2, 3, 1, 4", "1, 2, 3, 4", "3, 1, 2, 4"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The Constitution containing the Directive Principles took effect in 1950; the Kothari Commission reported in 1966; the national school-meal programme dates to 1995; and NLM was launched in 1988. The Kothari Commission was appointed earlier, in 1964.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 90; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 90, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 11, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": ""}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-091", "question": "The content of the passage is related to: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Evaluation", "Teaching", "Training", "Educational objectives"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The passage explains how educational objectives can be derived from contemporary life and expressed in terms of behaviour and content. Transfer of training supports this argument rather than being its sole subject.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 91; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 91, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "The effort to derive educational objectives from studies of contemporary life largely grew out of the difficulty of managing an increased body of knowledge which developed after the advent of the Industrial Revolution and kept on accelerating with each generation. One argument is that contemporary life is so complex and changing that we need to focus educational efforts upon its critical aspects, in view of the non-significance of knowledge important fifty years ago. The second argument grows out of findings relating to the transfer of training: educational objectives need to develop the several faculties of the mind, enabling students to use their trained minds to meet the conditions they encounter. Learners use similarities between life situations and learning situations to their advantage and are better placed to apply learning outside the training situation. A satisfactory formulation of objectives indicates the behavioural aspects and the content aspects, providing a clear specification of what the educational job is.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-092", "question": "The text argues that educational objectives need to evolve from: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["A historical base", "Research", "Experience", "Training"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The author appeals to studies of contemporary life and findings about transfer of training. “Research” best captures these systematic sources of evidence in the offered choices.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 92; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 92, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "The effort to derive educational objectives from studies of contemporary life largely grew out of the difficulty of managing an increased body of knowledge which developed after the advent of the Industrial Revolution and kept on accelerating with each generation. One argument is that contemporary life is so complex and changing that we need to focus educational efforts upon its critical aspects, in view of the non-significance of knowledge important fifty years ago. The second argument grows out of findings relating to the transfer of training: educational objectives need to develop the several faculties of the mind, enabling students to use their trained minds to meet the conditions they encounter. Learners use similarities between life situations and learning situations to their advantage and are better placed to apply learning outside the training situation. A satisfactory formulation of objectives indicates the behavioural aspects and the content aspects, providing a clear specification of what the educational job is.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-093", "question": "According to the passage, the Industrial Revolution resulted in: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Accelerating growth in the body of knowledge", "Slow growth in the body of knowledge", "No change in the body of knowledge", "Confusion in knowledge development"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The passage explicitly connects the Industrial Revolution with an expanding body of knowledge that accelerated with successive generations.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 93; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 93, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "The effort to derive educational objectives from studies of contemporary life largely grew out of the difficulty of managing an increased body of knowledge which developed after the advent of the Industrial Revolution and kept on accelerating with each generation. One argument is that contemporary life is so complex and changing that we need to focus educational efforts upon its critical aspects, in view of the non-significance of knowledge important fifty years ago. The second argument grows out of findings relating to the transfer of training: educational objectives need to develop the several faculties of the mind, enabling students to use their trained minds to meet the conditions they encounter. Learners use similarities between life situations and learning situations to their advantage and are better placed to apply learning outside the training situation. A satisfactory formulation of objectives indicates the behavioural aspects and the content aspects, providing a clear specification of what the educational job is.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-094", "question": "What studies does the author propose in the passage? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Sentiments", "Contemporary life studies", "The training system", "Future requirements"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The opening argument proposes deriving objectives from studies of contemporary life so that education addresses significant present conditions.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 94; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 94, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "The effort to derive educational objectives from studies of contemporary life largely grew out of the difficulty of managing an increased body of knowledge which developed after the advent of the Industrial Revolution and kept on accelerating with each generation. One argument is that contemporary life is so complex and changing that we need to focus educational efforts upon its critical aspects, in view of the non-significance of knowledge important fifty years ago. The second argument grows out of findings relating to the transfer of training: educational objectives need to develop the several faculties of the mind, enabling students to use their trained minds to meet the conditions they encounter. Learners use similarities between life situations and learning situations to their advantage and are better placed to apply learning outside the training situation. A satisfactory formulation of objectives indicates the behavioural aspects and the content aspects, providing a clear specification of what the educational job is.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-095", "question": "Similarity between life situations and training situations helps in: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Improved learning", "No change in learning", "Slow learning", "Non-behavioural training"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "Similarity supports transfer: learners can use what they have learned in situations beyond training. This is the passage’s reason for relating learning to real life.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 95; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 95, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "The effort to derive educational objectives from studies of contemporary life largely grew out of the difficulty of managing an increased body of knowledge which developed after the advent of the Industrial Revolution and kept on accelerating with each generation. One argument is that contemporary life is so complex and changing that we need to focus educational efforts upon its critical aspects, in view of the non-significance of knowledge important fifty years ago. The second argument grows out of findings relating to the transfer of training: educational objectives need to develop the several faculties of the mind, enabling students to use their trained minds to meet the conditions they encounter. Learners use similarities between life situations and learning situations to their advantage and are better placed to apply learning outside the training situation. A satisfactory formulation of objectives indicates the behavioural aspects and the content aspects, providing a clear specification of what the educational job is.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-096", "question": "According to the text, “broader issues” includes: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Basic education", "Primary education", "Basic and primary education", "Basic and primary education together with a focus on development issues"], "answer": 3, "explanation": "The passage says that the effort must extend beyond basic and primary education to broader development concerns, including the conditions that support women’s rights and empowerment.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 96; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: D.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 96, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "D", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "Though principles of participatory planning and decentralization have become de rigueur, it may be necessary to take them further than is customary, so as to ensure that flexibility, which is a hallmark of a relevant programme for adults, is retained. Another existing premise that may require unlearning is the clubbing together of basic education for adults and children, not only because the learning patterns of adults are distinct from those of children, but also because their needs and priorities express the experiences that constitute each individual history and resultant aspiration. None of these come cheap. In addition, the analysis indicates that a range of trained professionals is required to see the job through. An often non-existent pool of talent is not adequately appreciated and is therefore bound to have a short shelf-life. The other half of the problem is that encouraging and facilitating this resource requires an effort not just to promote basic and primary education, but also to focus on broader issues of development. This is most imperative in relation to women’s literacy and education: as long as women continue to lack social, political and personal rights, they will not benefit much from literacy interventions. Prevailing inequalities will most likely be reinforced systematically through the systems and people that design and implement programmes. Finally, a conspicuous absence of inspiring female role models will make the path to empowerment that much steeper.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-097", "question": "According to the text, what is the hallmark of a relevant programme for adults? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Rigidity", "Flexibility", "Enormity", "Authenticity"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The passage explicitly identifies flexibility as the hallmark. Participatory planning and decentralisation should be taken far enough to preserve that flexibility.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 97; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 97, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 12, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "Though principles of participatory planning and decentralization have become de rigueur, it may be necessary to take them further than is customary, so as to ensure that flexibility, which is a hallmark of a relevant programme for adults, is retained. Another existing premise that may require unlearning is the clubbing together of basic education for adults and children, not only because the learning patterns of adults are distinct from those of children, but also because their needs and priorities express the experiences that constitute each individual history and resultant aspiration. None of these come cheap. In addition, the analysis indicates that a range of trained professionals is required to see the job through. An often non-existent pool of talent is not adequately appreciated and is therefore bound to have a short shelf-life. The other half of the problem is that encouraging and facilitating this resource requires an effort not just to promote basic and primary education, but also to focus on broader issues of development. This is most imperative in relation to women’s literacy and education: as long as women continue to lack social, political and personal rights, they will not benefit much from literacy interventions. Prevailing inequalities will most likely be reinforced systematically through the systems and people that design and implement programmes. Finally, a conspicuous absence of inspiring female role models will make the path to empowerment that much steeper.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-098", "question": "Which statement about adult learning is correct according to the passage? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["It is similar to the basic education of children", "Adults and children have the same needs and priorities", "Adults’ needs and priorities differ from children’s", "Experience cannot distinguish children from adults"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "The text distinguishes adults’ learning patterns, needs and priorities from children’s and connects those differences to adults’ histories and aspirations.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 98; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: C.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 98, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "C", "page": 13, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "Though principles of participatory planning and decentralization have become de rigueur, it may be necessary to take them further than is customary, so as to ensure that flexibility, which is a hallmark of a relevant programme for adults, is retained. Another existing premise that may require unlearning is the clubbing together of basic education for adults and children, not only because the learning patterns of adults are distinct from those of children, but also because their needs and priorities express the experiences that constitute each individual history and resultant aspiration. None of these come cheap. In addition, the analysis indicates that a range of trained professionals is required to see the job through. An often non-existent pool of talent is not adequately appreciated and is therefore bound to have a short shelf-life. The other half of the problem is that encouraging and facilitating this resource requires an effort not just to promote basic and primary education, but also to focus on broader issues of development. This is most imperative in relation to women’s literacy and education: as long as women continue to lack social, political and personal rights, they will not benefit much from literacy interventions. Prevailing inequalities will most likely be reinforced systematically through the systems and people that design and implement programmes. Finally, a conspicuous absence of inspiring female role models will make the path to empowerment that much steeper.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-099", "question": "In the passage, the phrase “de rigueur” means: (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["The principle is not accepted", "The principle is accepted through force", "The principle is required", "The principle is not required"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "De rigueur means required or expected by established practice or convention. It does not mean accepted through force. C is therefore the closest option; the book’s printed B is incorrect.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 99; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: B. Book key: B; corrected answer: C. Cambridge Dictionary: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/de-rigueur", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 99, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "B", "page": 13, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "Book key: B; corrected answer: C. Cambridge Dictionary: https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/de-rigueur", "passage": "Though principles of participatory planning and decentralization have become de rigueur, it may be necessary to take them further than is customary, so as to ensure that flexibility, which is a hallmark of a relevant programme for adults, is retained. Another existing premise that may require unlearning is the clubbing together of basic education for adults and children, not only because the learning patterns of adults are distinct from those of children, but also because their needs and priorities express the experiences that constitute each individual history and resultant aspiration. None of these come cheap. In addition, the analysis indicates that a range of trained professionals is required to see the job through. An often non-existent pool of talent is not adequately appreciated and is therefore bound to have a short shelf-life. The other half of the problem is that encouraging and facilitating this resource requires an effort not just to promote basic and primary education, but also to focus on broader issues of development. This is most imperative in relation to women’s literacy and education: as long as women continue to lack social, political and personal rights, they will not benefit much from literacy interventions. Prevailing inequalities will most likely be reinforced systematically through the systems and people that design and implement programmes. Finally, a conspicuous absence of inspiring female role models will make the path to empowerment that much steeper.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}, {"id": "adult-2020-06-II-100", "question": "Which best depicts the difficulty in women’s empowerment described in the passage? (UGC NET, June 2020)", "options": ["Systematic perpetuation of inequalities", "Social rights are taken care of", "Political rights have no role", "Personal rights are extraneous factors"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "The author warns that inequalities may be reproduced by programme structures and implementers. Social, political and personal rights are important to empowerment, so the other options contradict the passage.", "reference": "June 2020 supplied paper, question 100; examination held 24 September 2020. Book key: A.", "section": "June 2020 — Paper II", "originalNumber": 100, "examSession": "June 2020", "examDate": "2020-09-24", "paper": "II", "printedAnswer": "A", "page": 13, "unscored": false, "keepOptionOrder": true, "qualityNote": "", "passage": "Though principles of participatory planning and decentralization have become de rigueur, it may be necessary to take them further than is customary, so as to ensure that flexibility, which is a hallmark of a relevant programme for adults, is retained. Another existing premise that may require unlearning is the clubbing together of basic education for adults and children, not only because the learning patterns of adults are distinct from those of children, but also because their needs and priorities express the experiences that constitute each individual history and resultant aspiration. None of these come cheap. In addition, the analysis indicates that a range of trained professionals is required to see the job through. An often non-existent pool of talent is not adequately appreciated and is therefore bound to have a short shelf-life. The other half of the problem is that encouraging and facilitating this resource requires an effort not just to promote basic and primary education, but also to focus on broader issues of development. This is most imperative in relation to women’s literacy and education: as long as women continue to lack social, political and personal rights, they will not benefit much from literacy interventions. Prevailing inequalities will most likely be reinforced systematically through the systems and people that design and implement programmes. Finally, a conspicuous absence of inspiring female role models will make the path to empowerment that much steeper.", "passageNote": "Passage punctuation and grammar lightly normalised from the supplied scan."}], "releaseStatus": "INCOMPLETE REVIEW DRAFT — NOT THE FULL v1.6 RELEASE"});
